Bugfix: A disturbed Redis no longer breaks requests when used as a cache

With Redis as memcache.local or memcache.distributed, every request reads
cached app information while starting up. When Redis refused or dropped the
connection - a restart, a network hiccup, maxclients reached under load - this
already threw an exception, and WebDAV answered with 503 (or 599 when handling
the error failed as well). Under load this hit many parallel requests at once,
for example the PROPFIND requests a desktop client sends after a bulk upload.

Redis used as a cache is now treated like an empty cache when it fails: the
request goes on without it, and the cause is logged once per request. Redis
used for file locking (memcache.locking) still has to be available. The
troubleshooting guide lists the usual causes of 503 under load.
